# Cross-model delegation

When this session runs inside T3 Code, delegate work that needs another model through the T3 Code MCP server, which knows which providers and models can run right now and tracks the child task for you.

- This section applies only when the T3 Code MCP tools are available in this session and the work needs a provider other than your own or a model your native subagent tool cannot serve; otherwise, keep using the native subagent tool.
- Tools the session lists by name count as available even when the harness must load their schemas before the first call.
- The tools are `orchestrator_capabilities`, `delegate_task`, `task_status`, and `task_cancel`. A harness may expose them under a prefix such as `mcp__t3-code__`.
- When a skill tells you to discover, attest, or launch another harness for cross-model work, delegate through T3 instead, before any of the skill's own discovery or launch steps.
- Carry out the rest of the skill's procedure with the delegated result, and adapt where the skill assumes its own route, such as a receipt that marks a peer as independently verified.
- Before delegating, list the runnable providers and models with `orchestrator_capabilities`, and choose a provider other than your own, a model, and options that suit the task.
- Tell the user which provider and model you delegated to.
- Run every delegated task in full-access runtime mode.
- Write a self-contained task prompt with all the context, prior findings, and constraints the child needs, because the child receives none of your conversation.
- When the delegated work must not change files, such as a review, say so explicitly in the task prompt.
- Delegate long work asynchronously, then continue other work or end your turn instead of polling. Read `task_status` only when you need the result mid-turn.
- When a skill's review procedure turns on its cross-model pass, delegate that pass asynchronously in the same step that dispatches the skill's in-process reviewers, and merge its result when it returns; never add a cross-model pass the skill would not run.
- When a delegated task fails, reports a provider error, or shows no progress, cancel it with `task_cancel` and retry the same task on another runnable T3 provider; fall back to the skill's own cross-model method only when no T3 provider succeeds, and tell the user that you did.
- Verify a delegated result against the repository or other primary evidence before you rely on it or report it as fact.

# Review findings

Apply every finding that `ce-code-review` and `ce-simplify-code` report, whatever its severity, P2 and P3 included. A low-severity finding is still a defect in the change, and fixing it now costs less than tracking it.

- Fix P2, P3, and lower findings on the branch the same way you fix P0 and P1 findings, and verify each fix.
- Do not defer a finding to a residual list, a follow-up ticket, or a note in the pull request body.
- When a fix needs a decision only the user can make, ask with the question tool instead of deferring it.
- Leave a finding unapplied only when you have verified that it is wrong or cannot be applied, and report which one and why.

# Branch names

Before the first push of a branch, check whether its name describes the change, and rename it locally with `git branch -m` when it does not. A tool-generated placeholder, such as a worktree codename like `hyperlapse122/mooneye` or `worktree-memoized-juggling-hartmanis`, or a random-hex branch like `t3code/1a2b3c4d`, does not describe a change. A descriptive name that keeps an owner prefix a tool added, such as `hyperlapse122/add-git-lfs-package`, is renamed as well when the project documents no rule that uses that prefix. Any other name that already describes the change stays.

- Rename only a branch that has never been pushed: no remote branch of the same name exists and no pull request uses it. A pushed branch keeps its name, even a placeholder. An upstream that points at another branch, such as `origin/main` set when the worktree was created, does not make a branch pushed. Push the renamed branch with `git push -u origin <new-name>` so the new name becomes its upstream.
- Never rename a branch onto, or push the renamed branch onto, a branch that already exists. Before renaming, check that the new name is free locally and on the remote with `git ls-remote --heads origin <new-name>`. When it is taken, or the push is rejected for a conflicting ref, pick another descriptive name instead of forcing it with `git branch -M`.
- Follow the project's documented branch-naming rule, from `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING`, the project docs, or a CI check or hook that validates branch names. A pattern seen only in existing branch names is not a rule.
- When the project documents no rule, name the branch `type/short-kebab-slug`: a Conventional Commits type such as `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, or `chore`, then a few lowercase hyphenated words that describe the change. This follows GitHub Flow's short, descriptive names, for example `feat/add-branch-naming-rule`. Drop an owner prefix a tool added, such as `hyperlapse122/`, and check the name with `git check-ref-format --branch`, which checks syntax only.
